// The Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W, reached through the C SDK -- a package, in the sense
// `design/packages.md` gives the word.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because this depends on nothing that resolves: what it needs is
// the pico-sdk, and the pico-sdk is not a sysl package and could not be one. It is a CMake build
// system that generates a second-stage bootloader, selects a board header and drives the final link
// with its own linker script -- none of which fits inside a thing `sysl build` fetches and compiles.
//
// So the arrangement is the other way up. **The SDK's CMake owns the link, and a program using this
// package is built with `sysl build-c` into an archive that CMake then links.** That is the one
// unusual thing about this package and everything else follows from it.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what arrived
// against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       pico2 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pico2", version = "0.0.9" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.0.9` on this repository. The coordinate names the *package*, `pico2`, while the module a
// program imports is `sh.sysl.pico2`; `packages.md § 9` keeps those two deliberately apart.

package {
  name    = "pico2"
  version = "0.0.9"
}

// **`heap = true`, and it was not always so.** Until 0.0.3 this package asked for nothing: every
// name here is a call into C that the SDK's own link satisfies, and a call into C is not a
// capability.
//
// What changed was `read_line`, which answered a `string` that grew as the line was typed rather than
// filling a buffer the caller had to size in advance. That editor now lives in `sysl.term.edit` and
// this package supplies the port it reads through — but the requirement stays, because the editor
// allocates and a program here reaches it. **It is a deliberate trade and not a slip.** The SDK links
// newlib, so this board has a heap whether or not this package touches one, and refusing to use it
// bought an API every caller had to work around. A target with genuinely no
// allocator wants different code -- and would want a different package anyway, since this one *is*
// the SDK's board and the SDK is what brings the heap.
//
// **No operating system, though, and there should never be one here.**
//
// **`headers` is the other kind of thing a package can need of its environment, and this is the case
// it was written for.** A capability is answered by the target and there is nothing for anybody to go
// and do; a header is answered by a path on a machine this package has never seen. The four C files
// here include ten headers none of them carries -- `pico/cyw43_arch.h`, `pico/time.h`, `cyw43.h`,
// four out of `lwip/`, and two out of mbedTLS and PSA -- and every one of them arrives in the SDK
// tree. So it is **one** name rather than three: all a consumer knows is a single `PICO_SDK_PATH`,
// and asking them to say the same thing under three names would be asking about how the SDK is laid
// out rather than about where it is.
//
// What it buys is the refusal. Without it, a consumer who has not wired their include paths through
// to `sysl build-c` finds out from clang, which names a header and knows nothing about sysl, this
// package, or the flag that would fix it:
//
//     fatal error: 'pico/cyw43_arch.h' file not found
//
// A path is deliberately not written here. This file is committed and describes the *package*, and
// where somebody keeps the SDK is not a property of the package -- `15 §8` refuses it in as many
// words, and it is the same split `@link` draws against `--link-path`.
requires {
  heap = true
  headers {
    pico_sdk = "the pico-sdk's headers, and the lwIP and mbedTLS ones that arrive in the same tree, which a CMake build knows as PICO_SDK_PATH"
  }
}

//
// **What it also does not do is carry a `@link` directive**, and the reason is different from every
// other package in the org. termbox2 has none because its implementation is vendored and compiled
// here; qcbor names `m` because it reaches `pow`. This one has none because the symbols arrive from
// a link line that CMake writes and sysl never sees. The absence looks the same and means something
// else, which is worth knowing when comparing the two.
